00:00:37.809 --> 00:00:40.260
Uh, let's go to mark from Newport.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:41.260
Mark from Newport.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:42.809
We haven't heard from for a while.

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:44.730
Uh, what's going on mark?

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:45.730
Okay.

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:46.730
Who?

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:47.730
David?

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:51.890
Um, so I had actually posted about this on
your sub Reddit, but I wanted to try to dig

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:54.210
a little deeper over the phone if possible.

00:00:54.210 --> 00:01:00.449
So you had mentioned that as opposed to Elizabeth
Warren's wealth tax, you had preferred alterations

00:01:00.449 --> 00:01:06.090
in the way capital gains are taxed as a means
to tax accumulated capital.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:12.220
So as a financial advisor, that just seems
like an inefficient way to tax accumulated

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:14.250
capital, which I really want to.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:16.400
So I was just curious, what am I missing?

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:21.760
Well, how, how would you like to tax it?

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:27.340
I would prefer something along the lines of
Elizabeth Warren's wealth tax, just because

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:34.890
there's not as many ways that people like
me are required to basically help people avoid

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:35.890
that tax.

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:43.980
If you read into her proposal, it's pretty
much just every dollar over 50 million, um,

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:45.250
with nothing really excluding.

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:50.360
Now I would be open to the conversation of
excluding certain types of assets from that

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:55.700
or if you're forced to draw assets, maybe
avoiding them from other types of taxation

00:01:55.700 --> 00:02:01.190
or, I don't know, I'm open to that conversation,
but I just as I think as a means as to have

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:08.179
a broader tax base to bring in more revenue
and just to to limit wealth inequality.

00:02:08.179 --> 00:02:12.810
I think there's less room for people like
me to help people avoid paying taxes.

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:17.519
Yeah, I mean I think that you have to balance
every single one of these proposals with the

00:02:17.519 --> 00:02:24.000
path to making it a reality and a number of
what seemed to be like pretty good legal analyses

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.569
that I've read say that passing a wealth tax,
like what she is proposing, whether or not

00:02:28.569 --> 00:02:33.139
you exclude certain types of assets would
be extraordinarily difficult and it could

00:02:33.139 --> 00:02:35.930
take a decade while it's hung up in courts.

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:40.670
So I think there's simpler things that could
be done that would be legally less complex

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:41.959
in order to raise revenue.

00:02:41.959 --> 00:02:46.170
I mean one of them is, and this is usually
thought of merely in terms of the context

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:51.920
of social security's viability, but you could
raise the income cap on social security from

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:56.310
whatever it is now, 123,000 I believe, to
something higher than that.

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:58.569
You don't have to put any new infrastructure
in place.

00:02:58.569 --> 00:03:02.769
You're literally just changing the number
by a different amount than you would by inflation

00:03:02.769 --> 00:03:07.620
alone and you immediately generate significantly
more revenue when it comes to capital gains.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:11.940
You are right that there are whole industries
built around helping people avoid capital

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:15.239
gains, but there are also changes that can
be made at the most drastic, which would have

00:03:15.239 --> 00:03:20.749
a lot of opposition would be taxing capital
gains at the same rate as ones a wage would

00:03:20.749 --> 00:03:22.470
be tax based on their income.

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:26.060
Um, that would be really do realize the game.

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:27.900
I'm sorry, what was that?

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:29.760
Oh, I'm sorry to interrupt.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:35.069
You still get to choose when you realize that
game and then you can still like Orient your

00:03:35.069 --> 00:03:36.069
investment allocation.

00:03:36.069 --> 00:03:37.819
You can keep the games on there.

00:03:37.819 --> 00:03:39.340
There's no question about it.

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:40.380
There's no question about it.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:42.050
It's not a silver bullet for sure.

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:45.389
Um, but I think that when we think you're
thinking about all the right stuff, I think

00:03:45.389 --> 00:03:50.249
that the PR, what we should really think about,
even if we like the wealth tax in principle

00:03:50.249 --> 00:03:54.779
is the reality that legislatively it would
be extremely difficult.

00:03:54.779 --> 00:03:56.451
That's not a reason not to think about it.

00:03:56.451 --> 00:03:57.590
It's not a reason not to do it.

00:03:57.590 --> 00:04:00.540
I just think there may be lower hanging fruit,
but it sounds like you're thinking along the

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.849
same lines.

00:04:02.849 --> 00:04:04.859
Yeah.

00:04:04.859 --> 00:04:11.359
The bottom line is that is accumulating capital
needs to be taxed, so whatever can get passed

00:04:11.359 --> 00:04:12.909
should get pass.

00:04:12.909 --> 00:04:14.569
All right mark, great to hear from you.

00:04:14.569 --> 00:04:15.569
Mark from Newport.

